## 13. Frequently Asked Questions

**Q: How often should my manager give me feedback?**  
A: Ongoing feedback should be provided in regular 1:1 meetings (weekly or bi-weekly). Real-time feedback on specific situations should be given as close to the event as possible. Formal feedback is gathered in mid-year check-in and annual review.

**Q: What if I disagree with my performance rating?**  
A: First discuss with your manager to understand their perspective and clarify the feedback. If you still disagree after that conversation, you can submit a formal appeal to HR within 7 days.

**Q: Can my rating be changed after the review?**  
A: Yes, during the calibration process (in December), HR may identify ratings that should be adjusted based on company standards. If an error is discovered (e.g., goals were misunderstood), HR can work with your manager to correct it. Once finalized and communicated, ratings are rarely changed unless there's a clear error.

**Q: Does my performance rating affect my job security?**  
A: Ratings of 1 or 2 indicate performance issues; employees receive additional support and coaching but may face termination if performance doesn't improve. Ratings of 3–5 indicate solid to exceptional performance and job security (barring business downturns). No employee is terminated purely for receiving a rating of 3.

**Q: How can I get a higher rating next year?**  
A: Set challenging goals aligned with team priorities, track progress throughout the year, ask for regular feedback, demonstrate company values, and communicate achievements. Discuss with your manager what would move you from your current rating to the next level.

**Q: Does my development plan guarantee a promotion?**  
A: No. A strong development plan and high performance increase the likelihood of promotion, but promotion also depends on available roles, business needs, and readiness as assessed by leadership. However, a good development plan will support your long-term career growth regardless of timing of promotion.

